analysisWe need to produce an assert statement for the comment.

We have a method add(int index, Object element) overriding ArrayList.add. The comment:

"// @@@ If the index is within the valid range (from 0 up to the initial size of the list), then for every element before the index, it either remains unchanged or continues to be null if it was null before."

Interpretation: Precondition: index is between 0 and old size inclusive? "up to the initial size of the list" likely means old size (size before addition). For add at index, valid range is 0 <= index <= oldSize. If index is within that range, then for all i where 0 <= i < index, the element at that position after the operation is either same as before, or if it was null before, it remains null. Actually "remains unchanged or continues to be null if it was null before" means that after addition, element at position i is equal to old element at i. If old element was null, it stays null (which is also equality). So basically for i < index, this.get(i) equals \old(this.get(i)). So we need an assert that for index in valid range, for all i from 0 to index-1, this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)))? Need to handle null: using == for null? In examples they used .equals for objects, but null case they'd need to handle null safely. In previous examples they used this.query(i).equals(\old(this.query(i))) which assumes non-null? In earlier spec they also used .equals. We'll follow similar pattern.

Thus assert: index >=0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < index; (this.get(i) == null ? \old(this.get(i)) == null : this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)))).

But they might accept simply this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))) because if both null, equals would throw NPE. Safer to use (this.get(i) == \old(this.get(i)))? But earlier examples used equals. We'll follow pattern: this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))). Might be okay.

Thus final assert:

assert index>=0 && index<=\old(this.size()) => \forall int i; 0<=i && i<index; this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)));

